---
import Base from '../../../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { extensionPaths } from '../../../../data/extension';

const paths = extensionPaths.en;
---

<Base
  lang="en"
  title="SEO & GEO Inspector Extension Privacy — Nacho Mascort"
  description="What data the SEO & GEO Inspector Chrome extension uses, where it is stored and what permissions it asks for."
  path={paths.privacy}
  alternatePath={extensionPaths.es.privacy}
  breadcrumbs={[
    { name: 'Home', path: '/en/' },
    { name: 'Tools', path: '/en/tools/' },
    { name: 'SEO & GEO Inspector extension', path: paths.landing },
    { name: 'Privacy', path: paths.privacy },
  ]}
>
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-5 pt-16 sm:px-6 sm:pt-20">
    <p class="meta"><a href={paths.landing} class="hover:text-ink">SEO &amp; GEO Inspector</a> · Privacy</p>
    <h1 class="display-1 mt-5">Extension privacy</h1>
    <p class="lede mt-6">Short version: the extension does not collect or send your data to any server of mine.</p>

    <div class="article-body mt-10">
      <h2>What data it uses and where it stays</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>The pages you visit.</strong> To show the status code and redirects, the extension reads the headers of
          the main page of each tab. That information is kept in Chrome's session memory and is deleted when you close the
          tab.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The content of the page you analyze.</strong> Only when you open the extension: headings, metadata, links
          and HTML are processed in your browser and are not sent anywhere.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Your DataForSEO credentials, if you add them.</strong> They are stored in Chrome's local storage, on your
          computer. They are used only to request volumes from DataForSEO from your browser. Volumes are cached for 30 days
          on your computer to avoid repeat queries.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Requests the extension makes</h2>
      <p>
        All of them leave your browser, over your connection, and their results stay on your computer: the robots.txt and
        the unrendered HTML of the page you analyze, the other language versions to check hreflang and, if you have set it
        up, the DataForSEO API.
      </p>

      <h2>What it doesn't do</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>It has no analytics, no cookies and no user identifiers.</li>
        <li>It doesn't sell or share data with anyone.</li>
        <li>It doesn't run code downloaded from the internet: all the code ships inside the extension.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Permissions</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Access to websites</strong>: read headers and redirects and download robots.txt, HTML and hreflang.</li>
        <li><strong>Active tab and scripting</strong>: read the page you analyze when you open the extension.</li>
        <li><strong>Web requests and navigation</strong>: record the main page's redirects.</li>
        <li><strong>Content settings</strong>: disable a site's JavaScript when you ask for it in JS vs HTML.</li>
        <li><strong>Network rules (declarativeNetRequest)</strong>: change a tab's user agent when you choose to browse as a bot, and let the page show inside the JS vs HTML comparison. Each rule only affects its own tab and is removed when you close it.</li>
        <li><strong>Storage</strong>: save your settings and the volume cache on your computer.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>When you uninstall it</h2>
      <p>
        Chrome deletes everything the extension stored on your computer and opens a page where you can tell me, if you
        want, why you are removing it. That form is optional and reaches me by email.
      </p>

      <h2>Contact</h2>
      <p>
        Data controller: Nacho Mascort, <a href="mailto:hola@nachomascort.com">hola@nachomascort.com</a>. For the website,
        the <a href="/en/privacy/">general privacy policy</a> still applies.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>
